#
# 17 SEPTEMBER 2026 — A DEFECT IN THIS FILE'S OWN TARGET LIST, found by being
# asked a plain question: «are you searching all the variations of a name?»
#
# For SURNAMES, yes — targets-2026-09-16.psv carries a «spellings to try»
# column and the trial proved why it matters (ZUBRINICH 191, ZUBRINIC 119,
# ZUBRINICK 10, all the same family).
#
# FOR GIVEN NAMES, NO. The target list hands the searcher whatever form the
# tree happens to hold, which for these parishes is very often the LATIN OF THE
# BAPTISMAL ENTRY — and sometimes the Latin ACCUSATIVE, because the priest
# wrote «baptizavi Gregorium». Nobody was ever entered in an American index in
# the Latin accusative. Tested:
firstname|blazevic firstname=josip|all|some|the Croatian form — returns records
firstname|blazevic firstname=joseph|all|some|the English form — returns records
firstname|blazevic firstname=josephus|all|0|THE LATIN FORM — NOTHING, and this is the form the target list gives
firstname|blazevic firstname=giuseppe|all|0|the Italian form — nothing either
# AND THE SITE'S OWN «Include name variants» CHECKBOX DOES NOT COVER IT. It is
# on by default and it still returns 0 for Josephus, so whatever it expands, it
# is not expanding across the languages a Croatian parish was written in.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS. 10 of the 386 target rows name a person in an oblique Latin
# case — Gregorium, Conradum, Joannem, Helenam, Georgium, Mathiam, Elisabetham,
# Joannam — and would return nothing for ever. A further 107 rows carry a given
# name that is in no variant group at all, so nothing tells the searcher what
# else to type. This archive HAD the answer the whole time: givennames.json has
# held 29 variant groups and 197 forms since the first week, and no search has
# ever consulted it.
#
# 20 SEPTEMBER 2026 — THE NATURALISATION SET, READ BY SURNAME RATHER THAN BY
# VILLAGE. The birthplace sweep had exhausted the villages; these are the
# archive's own surnames, whose counts the trial recorded and never opened.
#
# AND THE FIRST THING LEARNED IS THAT AN INDEX COUNT IS NOT A PEOPLE COUNT.
# «kalan» returns 20 rows and holds TWO HOUSEHOLDS. The rows are of three kinds
# and only one of them is a person worth opening:
#   Declaration of intention / Petition — the useful record: birth date, birth
#       place as transcribed, spouse, every child with a date.
#   Oath of allegiance — A NAME, A YEAR AND A CERTIFICATE NUMBER. No birth
#       date, no birth place, no household. Four were opened before the pattern
#       was clear; skip them.
#   Ids ending /1 /2 /3 /4 — relations named ON a record, not petitioners. Only
#       /0 is the applicant, which the trial had already written down.
surname|kalan|naturalisations|20|TWO households, NEITHER Croatian — see below
surname|kalan firstname=frank|naturalisations|3|Frank Kalan b.10 Apr 1873, «KRAIN, AUSTRIA» — Carniola, SLOVENIA
surname|kalan firstname=katherine|naturalisations|2|Katherine Kalan b.16 Jul 1893, «RUDNYKI, POLAND»
surname|vukelic|naturalisations|17|THREE men, one of them new — see below
surname|vukelic firstname=louis|naturalisations|4|Louis Peter Vukelić b.18 Mar 1892, «ROGUL, JUGO-SLAVIA» — NEW
#
# THE KALANJ LEAD IS DEAD, AND THAT IS WORTH AS MUCH AS A FIND. The trial's
# closing note recorded «kalanj 0, kalan 20» and left the 20 as a lead. They are
# a Slovene family from Carniola and a Polish family from Rudnyki. KALANJ IS THE
# LARGEST SURNAME IN THE TARGET LIST — 95 of 386 people — and the one record set
# on this site that indexes a birthplace holds not one of them. Nobody from
# Klenovica or Brinje filed a naturalisation petition in these courts.
surname|papich|naturalisations|13|7 petitioners; opened 3 — ZADAR and Herzegovina, not Senj
surname|blazevich|naturalisations|6|3 petitioners, ONE household — «Rutkishki, Russia»
#
# AND A CAUTION ABOUT THE «BIRTH COUNTRY» COLUMN, found by accident and worth
# more than the searches that found it.
#
# ONE MAN GIVES TWO DIFFERENT BIRTH COUNTRIES FIVE YEARS APART:
#   1919 declaration   Riedy Papich   b. 26 Mar 1885   «ZARA, AUSTRIA»
#   1924 petition      Rudolph Papich b. 26 Mar 1885   «ITALY»
# Same birth date, same Philadelphia court, consecutive record ids. Zara is
# ZADAR, and it passed from Austria to Italy at Rapallo in 1920 — between his
# two filings. So «birth country» in this index is THE COUNTRY THAT HELD THE
# PLACE WHEN THE CLERK WROTE, not when the person was born, and the same man
# will be found under two nationalities depending which paper you open.
#
# This archive already publishes a sovereignty timeline for exactly this reason.
# It now has an American index demonstrating it against one person's own name.
